<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayezid II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1447">1447</a>–<a href="/years/1512">1512</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Bayezid II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bayezid II was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1481 to 1512, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. His early reign was shaped by the rivalry of his brother Cem. He welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1492, took Kilia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Akkerman</a> from Moldavia in 1484, and in the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of 1499–1503 captured Lepanto, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a>. He was forced to abdicate by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> in 1512.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii.jpg" alt="Bayezid II" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Veronese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paolo_Veronese_(Nachfolger)_-_Sultan_Bajozeth_II._-_2246_-_Bavarian_State_Painting_Collections.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bayezid was born at Demotika, the first son of Şehzade Mehmed, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, and his concubine Gülbahar Hatun. His birth is dated 3 December 1447 or 1448. Around 1454 to 1456 he was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a> and held the post for 27 years, until he became the eighth Ottoman sultan. In 1473 he fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-otlukbeli">Battle of Otlukbeli</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His first concern was the quarrel with his brother Cem Sultan, who claimed the throne and sought backing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Defeated by Bayezid's armies, Cem sought protection from the Knights of St. John at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, who handed him to Pope Innocent VIII. The pope thought of using him to drive the Turks out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> never came about, and Cem died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Bayezid worked to keep domestic politics running smoothly, which earned him the epithet the Just. He returned to their former owners most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">waqf</a> properties Mehmed had taken for the state.</p><p>In 1484 he led a campaign against the Moldavian Black Sea ports of Kilia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Akkerman</a>, supported by Wallachian troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>. Kilia surrendered about 14 July 1484 and Akkerman fell in early August. A first Ottoman-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> war was fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a> from 1485 to 1491 over the southern Anatolian frontier. Rebellions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, often backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, troubled his reign.</p><p>In July 1492 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> expelled its Jewish and Muslim populations. Bayezid sent the navy under Kemal Reis to carry the refugees to Ottoman lands and ordered governors to receive them, threatening death for any who treated them harshly. Many settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Bayezid suffered from gout and left government largely to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>, while his sons Ahmed, Selim and Korkut rivalled each other. An earthquake on 10 September 1509, called the Little Apocalypse, killed several thousand people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. He abdicated in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> and died on 26 May 1512. Historians describe the reign as one of consolidation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayezid II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1484 <b>Sultan Bayezid II</b> captured Chilia and Cetatea Albă, cutting Moldavia off from the sea, and in 1497 Stephen beat a Polish invasion under John I Albert in the Cosmin Forest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottoman mosques emulated its style a thousand years later: the Şehzade Mosque, the Süleymaniye Mosque, the Rüstem Pasha Mosque and the <b>Bayezid II</b> Mosque among them.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The layout emulates the Hagia Sophia and the earlier <b>Bayezid II</b> Mosque: the central dome is flanked by semi-domes in front and behind, while a row of smaller, lower domes covers each lateral aisle, with large tympanas filled with windows between them.</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Only a son of the dynasty could reign, and the throne went to the prince who prevailed over his brothers, sometimes before his father’s death: Selim I forced his father <b>Bayezid II</b> to abdicate in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1500, during the Ottoman-Venetian war, <b>Sultan Bayezid II</b> stormed Modon after a long siege, and Coron surrendered soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-modon-and-coron">Venetian Modon and Coron</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Didymoteicho, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.35,26.5&amp;year=1489" aria-label="Didymoteicho on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 689 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bayezid II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 959h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 959h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.35,26.5&amp;year=1489">What was here in 1489? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
